      "section": "main",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:main}\nFor every $0<c_1<2/3$ and $2/3\\le c_2<1$, the parameters in this\nsection can be chosen so that $f$ in \\eqref{eq:objective} belongs to\n$C^2(\\R^2)$, has a globally H\\\"older continuous Hessian with exponent\n$1/2$, and satisfies $\\frac12I\\preceq\\nabla^2f(x)\\preceq\\frac32I$\nfor all $x\\in\\R^2$. With the positive definite initial matrix in\n\\eqref{eq:counterexample-initial-data} and positive step lengths in\n\\eqref{eq:counterexample-recurrence}, classical DFP is well defined and\nevery step satisfies \\eqref{eq:armijo} and \\eqref{eq:strong-curvature},\nbut $\\|\\nabla f(x_k)\\|\\longrightarrow G_\\infty>0$.\nThe same conclusion holds in every dimension $n\\ge2$.",
      "statementDependencies": [],
      "title": "DFP can fail under strong Wolfe conditions",
      "type": "Theorem",
      "paperMapping": "Main result, strengthened by the sharpness statement proved later in the paper.",
      "texLabel": "thm:main",
